    }
  },
  "decisive_biochemical_rationale": {
    "enzymological_definition": "A 'biochemical reaction' requires an active forward chemical transformation catalyzed by an enzyme converting defined substrates into distinct covalent products (Parent Drug + O2 + NADPH + H+ -> Arene Oxide + H2O + NADP+). 'Inhibition of glucuronidation' describes an enzyme kinetic state or clearance failure (the absence of a reaction).",
    "direction_of_toxicity": "Phase II glucuronidation is a protective detoxification pathway yielding benign, excretable conjugates; it prevents epidermal necrolysis. A protective clearance pathway cannot logically or biochemically be designated as the reaction that 'resulted in' blisters.",
